                                           cim:OilAnalysisFluidAnalogKind;
                                         rdfs:comment "Pour point (in �C). The pour point of a liquid is the temperature at which it becomes semi solid and loses its flow characteristics."@en;
                                         rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61968.ttl>;
                                         rdfs:label "pourPoint"@en;
                                         cimdoc:enumCode 15 .
cim:OilAnalysisFluidAnalogKind.pourPointAutomatic a owl:NamedIndividual,
                                                    cim:OilAnalysisFluidAnalogKind;
                                                  rdfs:comment "Pour point (in �C) determined by automatic method. The pour point of a liquid is the temperature at which it becomes semi solid and loses its flow characteristics."@en;
                                                  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61968.ttl>;
                                                  rdfs:label "pourPointAutomatic"@en;
                                                  cimdoc:enumCode 16 .
cim:OilAnalysisFluidAnalogKind.powerFactorPercent a owl:NamedIndividual,
                                                    cim:OilAnalysisFluidAnalogKind;
                                                  rdfs:comment "Fluid power factor at specified temperature (in percent). Commonly used in US. (Note: Fluid power factor is very similar to fluid dissapation factor, but fluid power factor is cosine of the phase angle between an AC voltage applied to oil and the resulting current (where dissipation factor is the tangent). For values up to 0.005, fluid power factor and dissapation factor are very close. There is a conversion equation between the two)."@en;
                                                  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://cimbology.com/ontology/iec61968.ttl>;
                                                  rdfs:label "powerFactorPercent"@en;
                                                  cimdoc:enumCode 3 .
